.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_CH 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_ch \- declare and create a hash map that hashes its keys with a custom procedure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_ch(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_hashing_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name. It also declares the companion
type variables. These variables carry key_t and val_t to every later
type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map that hashes its keys with
custom_hashing_proc, and not with the map's own hashing for key_t.

This macro is chmap_construct(3) plus a hashing procedure. It uses the
default allocator. chmap_construct_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead,
and chmap_construct_full(3) takes both. chmap_construct_ch_scoped(3) is
the same macro with a cleanup attribute that destroys the map at scope
exit.

A custom procedure changes only how the map turns a key into a number. It
does not change which implementation strategy the map picks from key_t and
val_t. It also does not change which keys the map considers equal.

chmap_declare(3) and then chmap_init_ch(3) is the two-step form. Use the
two-step form when the caller must handle a creation failure instead of
the failure being fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name              Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key
                     and, with val_t, which implementation strategy the map
                     uses
val_t                Value type
custom_hashing_proc  A ccol_hashing_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's own
                     hashing for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM HASHING"
.nf
A custom procedure gets a pointer to the key bytes and their length. It
returns an unsigned long. It must return the same value for two keys that
the map considers equal. For a char * key, equal means equal contents and
not an equal pointer. For a fixed-width key type, the length is the map's
own fixed key width. For a string or other buffer-like key, the length is
the byte length that the caller gave. The procedure can therefore always
hash exactly that number of bytes. It needs no NUL terminator and no length
prefix of its own.

The return value does not need to avalanche. The map finalizes the value
that a custom procedure returns with its own bit-mixing step. It does this
before it reduces the value to a slot or a bucket. A procedure that puts
all of its entropy in the low bits therefore still spreads across the
table. Examples are a sum, a small identifier and a truncated checksum.
The contract is only
"any function of the key that respects the map's notion of key equality".
There is no rule about which end of the result carries the entropy.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
Uses the default allocator and CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16)
buckets
The map canonicalizes a float or double key before the procedure sees it.
A custom procedure therefore never gets a raw -0.0
A long double key is the one case where a custom procedure changes key
handling. chmap_create_full(3) states what such a procedure has to do
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
chmap_destroy(3) destroys the map. The companion type variables follow the
scope of the handle, so a function that gets the handle needs
chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
unsigned long hash_bytes(const void *p, size_t size);

chmap_construct_ch(scores, char *, int, hash_bytes);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
